      "body": "## Summary\n\nFixes #61800. Thank you @RoelsJonas for the detailed report, reproducer, and compute-sanitizer output.\n\n`fused_recurrent_gated_delta_rule_fwd` allocated the non-inplace final state with `T` rows, but the kernel's non-inplace branch stores the recurrent state after every token at row `bos + i_t`, where `bos = i_n * T` for sequence `i_n` in `[0, N)` (`N = B` when `cu_seqlens is None`). Any fixed-length batch with `B > 1` therefore wrote up to row `N*T - 1` past the end of the `T`-row buffer — an out-of-bounds `__global__` write, as the reporter's compute-sanitizer output confirms.\n\nThe fix allocates `N * T` rows, matching the kernel's per-token write pattern. This also agrees with the initial-state load in the same kernel (`p_h0 = h0 + bos * HV * V * K`), which already assumes per-token rows across the batch (the reporter's `h0` has shape `(B*T, HV, V, K)`). In the variable-length path (`cu_seqlens` given), `N * T` rows likewise cover every `bos + i_t` write (the wrapper enforces `q.shape[0] == 1` with flattened inputs there). The in-place path and all kernel logic are untouched; for `B = 1` the allocation is byte-for-byte identical to before. All in-tree callers (`olmo_gdn_linear_attn.py`, `qwen_gdn_linear_attn.py`) pass `inplace_final_state=True`, so this branch is unreachable in vLLM itself — the fix only hardens the public op for out-of-tree users.\n\n**Not a duplicate:** checked open PRs for `61800 in:body` and for `fused_recurrent final_state` — no open PR addresses this.\n\n## Reproducer (adapted from @RoelsJonas's report; only the import path updated for current main)\n\n```python\nimport torch\nfrom vllm.third_party.flash_linear_attention.ops.fused_recurrent import fused_recurrent_gated_delta_rule\n\ntorch.manual_seed(0)\ndev = \"cuda\"\nB, T, H, HV, K, V = 2, 1, 1, 1, 16, 16\nq = torch.randn(B, T, H, K, device=dev)\nk = torch.nn.functional.normalize(torch.randn(B, T, H, K, device=dev), dim=-1)\nv = torch.randn(B, T, HV, V, device=dev)\ng = -torch.rand(B, T, HV, device=dev)\nbeta = torch.rand(B, T, HV, device=dev)\nh0 = torch.randn(B * T, HV, V, K, device=dev)\no, final_state = fused_recurrent_gated_delta_rule(q, k, v, g, beta, scale=K ** -0.5, initial_state=h0,\n                                                  inplace_final_state=False, use_qk_l2norm_in_kernel=True)\ntorch.cuda.synchronize()\n```\n\n**Before this fix** (verbatim from the issue report):\n\n```\n$ PYTORCH_NO_CUDA_MEMORY_CACHING=1 compute-sanitizer --tool memcheck python repro.py\n========= Invalid __global__ write of size 16 bytes\n=========     at fused_recurrent_gated_delta_rule_fwd_kernel ...\n=========     ... bytes after the nearest allocation\n```\n\n**After this fix:** the buffer now has `N*T` rows, so the same script is expected to run with no sanitizer errors. I could not execute the GPU reproducer myself — my environment has no GPU (see test notes below) — so GPU verification relies on the reporter's reproducer and CI.\n\n## Test results\n\nCommands run (CPU-only environment, no GPU available):\n\n- `python3 -m py_compile vllm/third_party/flash_linear_attention/ops/fused_recurrent.py` — passed\n- `ruff check vllm/third_party/flash_linear_attention/ops/fused_recurrent.py` — all checks passed\n- `ruff format --check vllm/third_party/flash_linear_attention/ops/fused_recurrent.py` — already formatted\n- Wrote and ran a CPU-side bounds-verification script replicating the kernel's exact `(bos + i_t)` index arithmetic for the non-varlen and varlen paths (including `B = 1`, empty-sequence early-return, and the `h0` load indexing). Results: old `T`-row allocation is out of bounds for `B > 1` non-varlen (max written row `N*T - 1 ≥ T`); new `N*T`-row allocation is in bounds in every case, and is identical to the old allocation for `B = 1`.\n\nNo existing unit tests cover this vendored op (no hits for `fused_recurrent_gated_delta_rule_fwd` under `tests/`); the affected triton kernel requires a GPU, which this environment lacks. Full verification of the reporter's sanitizer reproducer is left to CI/GPU reviewers.\n\n## Model evaluation\n\nNot applicable: the change only resizes the host-side allocation of a code path (`inplace_final_state=False`) that no in-tree vLLM caller exercises. Kernel math, the in-place path, and all model code paths are untouched, so no model output or serving behavior changes.\n\n## AI assistance disclosure\n\nThis contribution was prepared with AI assistance. The fix was verified by tracing the triton kernel's store indexing against the allocation for both the non-varlen and varlen paths, corroborated by the per-token initial-state load in the same kernel and by the reporter's `(B*T, HV, V, K)` `h0` shape. A human reviewed every changed line before submission.\n\nCo-authored-by: Muse Spark <muse-spark@meta.ai>\n",
